# Listen on every interface *inside the container*. The published port is bound
# to 127.0.0.1 on the host (see compose.yaml), so the only thing that can reach
# this is the host's own loopback.
SocksPort 0.0.0.0:9050

# Who is allowed to use it, from Tor's point of view inside the container.
#
# Loopback is here because Docker's port forwarder rewrites the source address:
# a connection from the host's 127.0.0.1 arrives at the container as 127.0.0.1
# too, not as the bridge gateway. Without this line Tor answers every request
# with "Denying socks connection from untrusted address 127.0.0.1", which from
# the client looks like a proxy that is up and refuses everything. The private
# ranges cover the bridge, which is how it arrives in other Docker networking
# modes.
SocksPolicy accept 127.0.0.0/8
SocksPolicy accept 172.16.0.0/12
SocksPolicy accept 192.168.0.0/16
SocksPolicy accept 10.0.0.0/8
SocksPolicy reject *

DataDirectory /var/lib/tor

# An onion service in front of the dev server.
#
# This is the only way to exercise ddIRC against a real onion address without
# borrowing someone else's: Tor will not route to a private address, so the
# server has to be reachable *as* a hidden service rather than through the
# SOCKS port. The target is the ircd container by name, which is why this
# service waits for that one.
#
# The address is derived from a key pair generated on first start and kept in
# the volume, so it survives a restart. `make dev-tor` prints it.
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/ircd
HiddenServiceVersion 3
HiddenServicePort 6697 ircd:6697

# Progress on stdout, so `make dev-tor-logs` shows bootstrap reaching 100% —
# the thing worth waiting for before pointing a client at it.
Log notice stdout
